    {
      "source": "Doctors of Running",
      "priority": 3,
      "url": "https://www.doctorsofrunning.com/2024/09/nike-pegasus-plus-review-2024.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: The full-length ZoomX midsole is moderately soft with good impact/shock absorption, but the heel can feel clunky at first and the forefoot may feel relatively low/firm for midfoot-forefoot strikers.",
        "Responsiveness: Energy return is moderate rather than explosive, with less bounce/snappiness than other ZoomX models; it can handle uptempo efforts but is more comfortable at easy-run paces.",
        "Stability: Rearfoot structure is decent (moderately stiff/rigid heel counter), but high midfoot volume and weak lockdown reduce lateral security and can cause foot translation/hot spots without aggressive lacing.",
        "Durability: Expected to be average overall, but early wear was reported by ~30 miles (lateral heel lug wear and exposed ZoomX tearing), and rough surfaces can accelerate outsole/foam damage."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Road Trail Run",
      "priority": 4,
      "url": "https://www.roadtrailrun.com/2024/08/nike-pegasus-plus-100-miles-review.html",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: ZoomX underfoot feels good but the ride is firmer than old Pegasus Turbo models, with less squish; despite that, long-run comfort and impact protection were strong (up to 19 miles) with no hot spots or blisters.",
        "Responsiveness: The shoe delivers noticeable spring/energy return, including late-run pop described as close to super-shoe-like, and a responsive yet controlled feel that helped hold tempo pace with less fatigue.",
        "Stability: Specific notes on torsional rigidity, heel counter stiffness, and lateral support are limited, but the platform was described as controlled and reliable across different run types and surfaces.",
        "Durability: At 100 miles there was no noticeable outsole or midsole wear, and the upper maintained structure and comfort with no visible breakdown."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "Believe in the Run",
      "priority": 5,
      "url": "https://believeintherun.com/shoe-reviews/nike-pegasus-plus-review/",
      "type": "qualitative",
      "keyFindings": [
        "Cushioning: Despite a 35/25 mm full ZoomX setup, the ride is reported as less cushioned than expected, with more ground feel and a somewhat thin forefoot feel.",
        "Responsiveness: The shoe is characterized as light and quick with a nimble ride that is easy to pick up, but not presented as exceptionally bouncy compared with newer premium trainers.",
        "Stability: Upper security is strong through the heel and midfoot (snug fit, padded heel counter), while the flexible forefoot and accommodating fit indicate moderate rather than highly rigid torsional/lateral support.",
        "Durability: The outsole uses plenty of rubber, which should help outsole longevity, but the large exposed ZoomX area may be more vulnerable to wear; no long-term wear results are provided."
      ]
    }
